ructions are correct.\n\nNow we need to check if any other k values are possible. The solution's argument for impossibility of k=2 and k≥4 seems plausible. However, we need to verify if there might be a construction for k=2 that the solution missed. Let's try to see if any configuration with k=2 exists for any n≥3. The solution's argument for k=2 uses the three rightmost columns and shows contradiction. Let's examine more thoroughly.\n\nWe have n lines, exactly 2 sunny lines. Non-sunny lines are vertical, horizontal, or slope -1. Horizontal and slope -1 lines meet each column at most one point, vertical lines meet each column at all points. So to cover columns with many points, we need vertical lines. The three rightmost columns have sizes 1,2,3. Since k=2, at most 2 points per column can be covered by sunny lines. So column n-2 (size 3) must have at least one point covered by a non-sunny line. Since non-sunny lines that are not vertical can cover at most one point per column (horizontal or slope -1), but they cannot cover a point in column n-2 and also cover other points in that column (since they would be vertical). So we need a vertical line x=n-2 to cover the column's points. So we have a vertical line x=n-2 among non-sunny lines.\n\nNow column n-1 (size 2) also must be covered. Since we have only 2 sunny lines, we could cover both points in column n-1 with the two sunny lines, each covering one point. However, we also have the vertical line x=n-2 covering column n-2. The vertical line x=n-2 does not affect column n-1. So we could have the two sunny lines covering the two points in column n-1. Then column n (size 1) point (n,1) must be covered. It cannot be covered by a sunny line together with a point from column n-1 because any line through (n,1) and (n-1,1) is horizontal (non-sunny), and line through (n,1) and (n-1,2) has slope -1 (non-sunny). So (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line that also passes through a point in column n-1. Could it be covered by a s